::ALG2_U04_L02_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>What factor represents 20% growth? {
= 1.20#Correct. Growth uses 1 + 0.20 = 1.20.
~ 0.20#This is only the rate.
~ 0.80#This represents 20% decay.
~ 2.00#This represents 100% growth, or doubling.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>What factor represents 7% decay? {
~ 1.07#This represents 7% growth.
= 0.93#Correct. Decay uses 1 - 0.07 = 0.93.
~ 0.07#This is only the rate.
~ 1.93#This does not model 7% decay.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A value starts at 600 and grows by 3% each year. Which model fits? {
~ A(t)=600(0.97)<sup>t</sup>#This models 3% decay.
~ A(t)=1.03(600)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses the parameters.
= A(t)=600(1.03)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. The initial value is 600 and 3% growth gives factor 1.03.
~ A(t)=600+3t#This is linear growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A value starts at 900 and decreases by 4% each year. Which model fits? {
~ A(t)=900(1.04)<sup>t</sup>#This models 4% growth.
~ A(t)=0.96(900)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses the parameters.
~ A(t)=900-4t#This subtracts 4 units per year, not 4%.
= A(t)=900(0.96)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. The initial value is 900 and 4% decay gives factor 0.96.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>In P(t)=1200(1.09)<sup>t</sup>, what is the percent growth rate? {
= 9%#Correct. A factor of 1.09 means 1 + 0.09, or 9% growth.
~ 1.09%#The decimal part 0.09 corresponds to 9%, not 1.09%.
~ 91%#That would match a factor of 1.91.
~ 109%#The factor is 109% of the previous amount, which means 9% growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>In V(t)=5000(0.82)<sup>t</sup>, what is the percent decay rate? {
~ 82%#That is the percent remaining, not the decay rate.
= 18%#Correct. A factor of 0.82 means 82% remains, so 18% is lost.
~ 0.82%#This treats the factor as the percent rate.
~ 118%#This does not represent decay from a factor of 0.82.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is A(2) for A(t)=100(1.5)<sup>t</sup>? {
~ 150#This is A(1), not A(2).
~ 300#This doubles the one-period increase incorrectly.
= 225#Correct. Compute 100 times 1.5 squared.
~ 250#This adds 150 instead of multiplying by 1.5 twice.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A laptop costs 1200 and loses 25% of its value each year. What is the factor? {
~ 1.25#This models 25% growth.
~ 0.25#This is the decay rate, not the remaining factor.
~ 1.75#This does not represent losing 25%.
= 0.75#Correct. If 25% is lost, 75% remains each year.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A bank account grows from 1000 by 6% annually. Which model fits? {
= A(t)=1000(1.06)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. The factor 1.06 represents 6% annual growth.
~ A(t)=1000(0.94)<sup>t</sup>#This models 6% decay.
~ A(t)=1.06(1000)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses initial value and factor.
~ A(t)=1000+6t#This adds 6 dollars per year, not 6%.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which factor indicates decay? {
~ 1.04#This indicates growth.
= 0.88#Correct. A factor between 0 and 1 indicates decay.
~ 1.25#This indicates growth.
~ 2.00#This indicates doubling growth.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which factor indicates growth? {
~ 0.15#This would shrink the amount to 15%.
~ 0.85#This indicates decay.
= 1.15#Correct. A factor greater than 1 indicates growth.
~ 0.50#This indicates decay by half.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A quantity decays by 30%. What percent remains? {
~ 30%#This is the percent lost, not the percent remaining.
~ 130%#This would represent growth.
~ 0.30%#This confuses percent and decimal form.
= 70%#Correct. If 30% is lost, 70% remains.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A quantity grows by 30%. What percent of the original is the new amount? {
= 130%#Correct. Growth by 30% means the new amount is 100% plus 30%.
~ 30%#This is only the increase.
~ 70%#This would be decay by 30%.
~ 0.30%#This confuses decimal and percent notation.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the initial value in C(t)=750(0.95)<sup>t</sup>? {
~ 0.95#This is the decay factor.
= 750#Correct. The coefficient is the starting amount when t = 0.
~ 95#This is the percent remaining.
~ 5#This is the decay rate in percent.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is the factor in M(t)=300(1.18)<sup>t</sup>? {
~ 300#This is the initial value.
~ 18#This is the percent growth rate, not the factor.
= 1.18#Correct. The factor is the repeated multiplier.
~ 0.18#This is the decimal rate, not the full factor.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which model shows 12% decay from an initial amount of 40? {
~ y=40(1.12)<sup>t</sup>#This models 12% growth.
~ y=12(40)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses parameters and uses the percent incorrectly.
~ y=40-12t#This is linear decrease.
= y=40(0.88)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Decay by 12% means a factor of 0.88.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which model shows 12% growth from an initial amount of 40? {
= y=40(1.12)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Growth by 12% means a factor of 1.12.
~ y=40(0.88)<sup>t</sup>#This models 12% decay.
~ y=12(40)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses parameters and uses the percent incorrectly.
~ y=40+12t#This adds 12 units each period, not 12%.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A medicine amount is modeled by M(t)=80(0.5)<sup>t</sup>. What does 0.5 mean? {
~ The amount increases by half.#A factor below 1 means decay, not growth.
= Half remains each time period.#Correct. A factor of 0.5 means the amount is multiplied by one-half each period.
~ The initial amount is 0.5.#The initial amount is 80.
~ The amount loses 0.5 units.#The model loses a percent, not a fixed 0.5 units.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A population model is P(t)=250(1.4)<sup>t</sup>. What is the growth rate? {
~ 4%#That would have factor 1.04.
~ 140%#The amount becomes 140% of previous, which means 40% growth.
= 40%#Correct. A factor of 1.4 means 1 + 0.4, or 40% growth.
~ 0.4%#This confuses decimal and percent notation.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A value changes by factor 0.60 each year. What is the decay rate? {
~ 60%#That is the percent remaining.
~ 6%#This misreads 0.60 as 6%.
~ 160%#This would not describe decay.
= 40%#Correct. If 60% remains, 40% is lost each year.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which situation is best modeled by exponential decay? {
= A car loses 18% of its value each year.#Correct. A constant percent loss each year is exponential decay.
~ A tank loses 18 gallons each hour.#A constant amount lost is linear.
~ A fee increases by 18 dollars each month.#A constant amount increase is linear.
~ A runner adds 18 minutes of training each week.#A constant amount added is linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>Which situation is best modeled by exponential growth? {
~ A savings jar gains 2 dollars each day.#A constant dollar increase is linear.
= A bacteria culture doubles every hour.#Correct. Doubling is repeated multiplication, so it is exponential growth.
~ A candle loses 2 cm each hour.#A constant amount decrease is linear.
~ A taxi fare adds 2 dollars per mile.#A constant amount per mile is linear.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>What is A(3) for A(t)=200(0.5)<sup>t</sup>? {
~ 100#This is A(1).
~ 50#This is A(2).
= 25#Correct. Compute 200 times 0.5 cubed, or 200 times 0.125.
~ 12.5#This is A(4).
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A model has factor 1.00. What does that mean? {
~ 100% growth.#A factor of 2 would represent 100% growth.
~ 100% decay.#A factor of 0 would represent 100% decay.
~ The initial value is 1.#The factor does not identify the initial value.
= No growth or decay.#Correct. Multiplying by 1 keeps the amount unchanged.
}

::ALG2_U04_L02_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U04_L02_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.EX.2; MLA.A2.EX.3<br><br>Question:<br>A house worth 300000 appreciates by 5% per year. Which model fits? {
= V(t)=300000(1.05)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Appreciation is growth, and 5% gives factor 1.05.
~ V(t)=300000(0.95)<sup>t</sup>#This models depreciation by 5%.
~ V(t)=1.05(300000)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses parameters.
~ V(t)=300000+5t#This adds 5 dollars per year, not 5%.
}

